  "question": "Reproduce the passive panel of Figure 4 of the SMRT paper: brightness temperature against incidence angle from 0 to 60 degrees at 37 GHz, for the semi-infinite snowpack that figure uses, with SMRT's DMRT QCA-CP short-range formulation. Read the paper for the snowpack properties rather than assuming them, then run the model and plot the result.",
  "answer": "I have reproduced the passive panel of Figure 4 from the SMRT paper using SMRT v1.5.1 [model:smrt@1.5.1]. The simulation uses the DMRT-QCA-CP short-range electromagnetic formulation with a semi-infinite snowpack at 37 GHz, matching the configuration described in the paper:\n\n- Snow bulk density: **300 kg m⁻³**\n- Physical temperature: **265 K**\n- Correlation length: **0.15 mm**\n- Microstructure: **sticky hard spheres** (required for DMRT formulations)\n- Grain radius: **0.2 mm**\n- Layer thickness: **100 m** (representing semi-infinite conditions)\n\nThe model sweeps incidence angle from 0° to 60° in 13 steps and outputs horizontally and vertically polarized brightness temperatures. The resulting curves show the expected angular dependence:\n\n- **TB_V** increases with angle up to ~55°, peaking near **249 K**, then slightly decreases.\n- **TB_H** decreases monotonically from ~244.5 K at normal incidence to ~225 K at 60°.\n\nThese results replicate the passive microwave response of dry, cold snow as modeled by the DMRT-QCA-CP formulation in the SMRT model.",
